    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with **Corbin Carroll** (79.02) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (76.91) serving as our offensive pillars. **Corbin Carroll** remains an auto-start despite the presence of Holmes on the hill; his ability to exploit the dynamic platoon advantage outweighs the 4% stolen base suppression caused by the pitcher\u2019s hold mechanics. Meanwhile, **Tyler Soderstrom** receives a massive +16% park factor bump, making him an elite play in the middle of the order. **Trevor Story** (71.91) and **Josh Naylor** (71.44) round out our top-tier targets. **Josh Naylor** is particularly well-positioned today against Kay, benefiting from a +19% SP skill adjustment and a helpful wind-out factor, which compensates for his unfavorable platoon split.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Austin Riley** (46.88), who narrowly missed the cut. While **Austin Riley** possesses the pedigree, the combination of a tough matchup against Blake Snell and a projected #7 spot in the order tanked his efficiency score, leaving him as a high-floor alternative should any of our starters be scratched. We are keeping a close eye on **Brice Turang**, **Jose Caballero**, **Dalton Rushing**, and **Seiya Suzuki**, as all four currently rely on \"Lineup Pending\" assumptions. **Mickey Moniak** (66.49) remains a high-upside play against Nola; while we expect a slight hit to his base-stealing potential due to Nola's elite hold, his +14% platoon edge and favorable spot in the order keep him firmly in the \"Strong\" category. \n\nWe are avoiding over-exposure to Spencer Strider, though **Dalton Rushing** maintains a marginal start status (58.70) due to his +17% SP skill adjustment and the fact that he is currently the best available option at the C2 slot. We will continue to monitor the wind and lineup confirmations for **Cole Young**, who stands to gain or lose significantly based on his final spot in the order. With the scarce nature of speed 